    <div id="ha-tree"></div>
    <h4 style="margin-top:20px">Issues Found</h4>
    <div id="ha-issues"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <details>
    <summary>How many H1 tags should a page have?</summary>
    <p>Best practice is <strong>exactly one H1</strong> per page. The H1 should describe the main topic of the page and ideally include your target keyword. While HTML5 technically allows multiple H1s, search engines still treat the first one as the primary heading.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Is it bad to skip heading levels (e.g., H1 → H3)?</summary>
    <p>Yes. Skipping levels breaks the document outline and confuses both search engines and assistive technologies. Always follow a logical hierarchy: H1 → H2 → H3 → H4, nesting subtopics under their parent sections.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Do headings affect SEO rankings?</summary>
    <p>Headings are a minor ranking signal, but they significantly impact content structure and readability. Well-structured headings help search engines understand your content hierarchy and can improve featured snippet eligibility.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Should I put my target keyword in the H1?</summary>
    <p>Yes. Your H1 should naturally include your primary target keyword. This reinforces topical relevance for both users and search engines. However, write naturally — don't force the keyword if it makes the heading awkward.</p>
  </details>
</section>

